{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED — bounded P1c correction publication review of source 3ad487624f0537fbb4199a822a762a5a3d8cc917, tree e20ac8af9e940f9936e88a98c426dfa3922ba181. This verdict covers all 33 exact-path artifacts attached to request437 (55b718657d303b31c64863ff85680251e0edc164) under my promise34aca61183f58879be8ed4bd1eebd9a706738ed1. I verified clean HEAD before and after execution, matched the complete artifact map to the 33-file diff from landed c254887d4cb365bbc7ce6a8fe8b1959e34328be5, and inspected all 11 changed files since my full review400 of398ccbe6. The other22 files are byte-identical; the full prior code, source, evidence, crypto, request350 and prefix-direction363 review coverage is retained. I inspected the actual prior changes verdict a45d17fa552a82a70dd1dde4b88298fa8b30bacc and its effective requester ratification bf5bc9a1ad3ab02c9e654bfecf7df3338ea0b494. No source/native edits, installs beyond requested npm ci, landing, push, adoption or self-ratification.\n\nTwo material ordinary-operation failure paths remain. Both were reproduced with a transparent localhost WebSocket proxy forwarding authentic fixture inputs to the exact real strfry binary and suppressing only EOSE for one chosen backend readback. The relay accepted/stored the original event; no process crash, filesystem corruption, invented crypto, backend replacement or production deployment assumption was involved.\n\nP1C-04 [P2, publication blocker] Reserve uncertain native writes before publication, not after successful readback.\nEvidence: tests/protocol-feasibility/gateway.ts:75–81 checks journalBytes but only inserts the retained identity/increments the counter after both awaited backend operations succeed. The surrounding command handler catches a readback failure and continues the serial tail (66–67,138–144); later writes can reuse the same capacity. The comment at79 says reserve/check but there is no reservation. Reconstructing at45–54 can count only inventory entries that were actually recorded. This contradicts the requested aggregate guarantee and docs/relay-profile.md:148–158.\nActual scenario: at the same injected logical quota boundary used by F08, remaining capacity was1,366 bytes. OBJECT A was accepted by strfry and its actual EVENT readback arrived, but EOSE was suppressed, so the gateway timed out. A was absent from the inventory and the counter remained268,434,090. Different equally sized OBJECT B then succeeded, taking the counter to268,435,456. Direct native REQ retrieved BOTH A and B (2,732 newly retained bytes for1,366 available). Retrying A now failed retention-full, and intact file reconstruction still omitted A. Serializing requests prevents concurrent races but does not repair this sequential uncertain-result case.\nResolution: conservatively reserve each new exact identity and encoded byte cost in bounded persisted state BEFORE sending it to the backend. Keep reserved/uncertain and verified-retained status distinct; only readback can support retained-success/coverage claims. A failed/unknown publish/readback must not release the allowance, and exact retry must reuse the reservation even at the cap. Recompute both reserved and retained usage on intact reconstruction; preserve older declarations and duplicate semantics. Refund only after an explicit safe reconciliation establishes non-retention, or simply retain the conservative charge in this bounded fixture. A documented fsync/P5 deferral does not excuse losing a reservation while the same live process handles the next request.\nRequired regression: actual native accept + withheld ACK and/or readback/EOSE, next distinct object refused, original exact retry repairs without second charge, intact reconstruction conservatively retains the uncertainty, and native identities/counters agree. Cover the shared helper for EVENT/OBJECT/CLOSURE; successful storage and public completeness remain separate from reservation.\n\nP1C-05 [P2, publication blocker] Preserve learned contiguous revocation when retention fails.\nEvidence: gateway.ts:93 validates the next entry's predecessor and owner/admission transition, but94 awaits retain before preserving that signed control or changing any read fence. Only the out-of-order branch92 records pending control knowledge. On quota failure, write failure or readback failure at94, the EVENT handler138 returns failure while the old roster remains readable, and the unchanged state file forgets the newly verified removal. This is a different path from the successfully corrected out-of-order reconstruction case.\nActual scenario: Bob was admitted in retained entry1. The authentic owner removal/Commit at contiguous entry2 passed entry/publicTransition validation and was accepted by native strfry; suppressing only its readback EOSE caused the operator reply to fail. The gateway reported readable=true with pending=[] and retained tip1. A newly connected, AUTHed Bob then received the original entry1 plus EOSE. Intact reconstruction also reported readable=true. This is known revocation being discarded, not the expressly accepted exposure to a revocation still unseen on an unreachable primary.\nAuthority: ratified direction363 explicitly says that once a removal is verified the subject is denied for subsequent output, requires a policy for verified revocation before contiguous retention catches up, and permits conservative denial. The normal successful-path cutoff described at docs/relay-profile.md:96 is insufficient for this failure path.\nResolution: after verifying a potentially authority-changing next control, preserve its authenticated identity/control knowledge and establish a conservative read fence BEFORE the first fallible/asynchronous retention operation. Do not falsely advance the retained frontier. Leave the fence/known control recoverable until exact retention/dependency reconciliation applies the transition, including quota-full and backend timeout paths; cancel unsent queues at learning/send boundaries. A full bounded pending buffer must also fail closed if newer valid control knowledge cannot be represented. Preserve existing old-tip/no-rollback, owner control binding, gap/fork/half-control, stale-restore and exact retry rules.\nRequired regression: with a nonempty old prefix, delay/drop removal retention acknowledgement/readback (and exercise logical capacity refusal), then prove no old history, closure, reconnect or queued/live release to Bob; reconstruct the intact file and retain denial; retry/fill the exact entry and prove final removal while an admitted member can resume. Capture the distinction between previously handed socket bytes and newly released bytes. No production fsync implementation is requested.\n\nDispositions of earlier findings and added scope:\n- P1C-01's exact out-of-order case is corrected: signed bounded pending events persist before buffered reply, reconstruct through signature/position validation, conservatively disable reads, and remain fenced through old-cursor/closure/reconnect until the missing dependency produces the proper roster. The independently rerun F06 verifies the nonempty-prefix scenario, plus intact/stale-restore external high-water, gaps/half-controls/forks. P1C-05 above is the remaining contiguous-failure counterpart; do not describe all learned-revocation cases complete yet.\n- P1C-02's happy paths are corrected: all three routes call one exact-identity helper, superseded declarations remain charged, successful readback is required, duplicates are uncharged, and reconstruction recomputes the full retained inventory. Actual F08 verifies native identities, refused IDs absent, three routes, retries and old declarations. P1C-04 above is the remaining uncertain-write gap.\n- P1C-03 is resolved: local NIP-11 metadata and gateway parser/socket coverage, separately AUTHed stopped replication child and deferred production behavior are correctly distinguished. Archive wrapping-key capability and immutable owner signing/attestation authority are separated; the synthetic founder's deliberate same-scalar device/NIP44 reuse is explicit. RFC9420 MLS1.0/version1 and suite1 are documented and machine-profiled. The pinned source remains the authority, not an unverified ecosystem compatibility claim.\n- Added archive-capacity-boundaries is real in both runtimes:128 actual ordered entries archive/verify,129 refuses; exactly16,777,216 canonical plaintext bytes AES-GCM roundtrip and16,777,217 refuses. The flat base64 alphabet/padding scan plus canonical re-encode rejects malformed/interior/excess padding and nonzero padding bits while avoiding the grouped-regexp stack overflow; chunked character encoding preserves bytes and full-bound execution. These are entry-count and generic encrypted-export dimensions, not a claim that the128-entry semantic archive is16MiB or that a relay can transport a16MiB single event.\n- G2-LONG-HISTORY is now named in docs, machine profile and always-failing gate diagnostic. The current archive can still refuse a longer admitted journal; no128-entry instance lifetime, Plan001 workload reduction, segmented format or full G2 pass was selected. Root's separate read-only composition assessment402 is intentionally outside this correction; its completion is not a prerequisite to publishing an honestly bounded correction. Actual composition and acceptance evidence remain an adoption prerequisite; large resumable transfer/performance/deployment remain P5/P6 work as specified.\n- Existing trust/custody scrutiny stands: message-specific genuine sender/application AEAD openings, original order/ciphertext/MLS/account proof correspondence and definition closure verification; historical control/public-context validity explicitly owner-attested over the full promised interval, not independently replayed historical Commits. Shared history excludes ongoing MLS/ratchet/owner vault secrets; separately keyed vault restores declared all-device-loss state. Old keys do not become erasable through rewrapping; immutable owner compromise and the selected unseen-removal old-history exposure remain explicit. Real welcome/removal/non-removal controls, provisional echo/interleaving, Node/Chromium, native isolation/filter/COUNT/NEG and exact source provenance coverage from400 remain applicable. None of these retained positives removes the two concrete gateway failure findings.\n\nIndependent execution and retained evidence (paths relative to the clean protocol-feasibility worktree):\n1. npm ci, npm run check, npm run build, and NOSEQ_STRFRY_ROOT=/Users/hughpyle/Documents/Codex/2026-09-06/the-atseq-project-is-progressing-well/work/noseq-strfry-native npm run probe:protocol-feasibility all exited0. Logs: artifacts/protocol-feasibility/reviewer-fix-verification/a9545e00-3dd6-4c8b-b226-57afa8199bba/{0,1,2,3}.log and commands.json.\n2. Full run artifacts/protocol-feasibility/runs/fe8092d7-0315-4a68-bf12-5092400551cd/evidence.json SHA25638d636a0e474d6ab64d10c03ad301baa6e3a491d14aa3e90539331bd046cca45:101 passed =12 P0 +33 unchanged P1a +16 unchanged P1b +16 P1c Node +16 actual Chromium +8 actual gateway cases, plus unchanged actual native size/COUNT observations. Node26.8.1/npm11.19.0, Chromium153.0.8010.12/revision1243, workerd1.20260903.1, compatibility2026-09-03, darwin arm64. Harness prerequisite cc710e80-05e2-438d-8329-b2c03ebe5210 SHA841133ddc575611ca88c30ce5b24ee2853b20bd57f0f8f14b2c670f00ad65f28; ordered prerequisite78803129-327d-4718-a46a-7a866dbf7456 SHAdd26b0c34d1713e4bfc74ab3c9cfe2a9077314c40fc47630c90fca994deff3e5.\n3. Extra actual-native fault experiment artifacts/protocol-feasibility/reviewer-fix-experiments/d8d3065a-81d3-4115-9e4d-92e2e4251988/result.json SHA2565b76f6b349ef0e0bdf2007615ee084cfaa966a6a924da5c8adc04935b0b083ad; sibling UUID.mjs is the exact reviewer scratch driver, and trace.json/run.log/native config/exit are retained. It uses the existing reviewer esbuild TypeScript loader, source imports unchanged. The quota available-byte counter injection matches the candidate F08 technique; it is not a physical disk-full claim. Both failure paths are actual sockets with exact synthetic signed/encrypted input and native persistence/readback. All child sockets/relays stopped.\n4. Eighteen independent public-verifier/closed-gate outcomes matched: artifacts/protocol-feasibility/reviewer-fix-negative/2dd6950a-c067-4c95-8874-6c96d819c238/results.json SHA256d11e25e229e030cbfc0bb7864a6e53f045f1cf665a3cb7e21386f0f52a047ed6. Valid source-bound copy passes; invented approval/gate/conclusion, omitted/skipped new capacity cases, duplicate case, wrong profile/source, missing prerequisite, altered raw report/F06 learned-control file/F08 quota file and absent native build record fail. Both full gate commands fail on valid evidence and fake override. Mutations were confined to new ignored evidence copies; all originals preserved.\n5. Profile SHA256353f8f3c26c8a021c743ca63fe286b0ba1835cbae99902a7daef5b9875dba167. strfry remains source4cd3cf64850caf47dda46c2a2abbbf3525a64d10/binarySHA028f26b98b0b895ed4d32f83642e3fa25173f3498330e9bfc2567a3fc06ffab4; its previously built native directory was executed read-only, not rebuilt. The request's builder success dbad6e2a,34-outcome negative setcadc7caf and all attached earlier failures (relative native directory, grouped base64 overflow, old collector and NEG route classification, reviewer experiments) remain preserved, not retroactively relabeled complete evidence.\n\nRecommendation: correct P1C-04 and P1C-05 within the bounded gateway fixture, retain failed-attempt evidence, and request a new clean exact-head independent review covering the refreshed artifacts. The gate/pending-documentation changes are appropriate. Publication approval at this head is withheld solely for the two concrete failure paths, not for demanding production P5/P6 or prematurely closing G2-LONG-HISTORY. G1–G5 remain UNPASSED and no production profile is adopted.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:77aeeeb3